==Problem==

A birdbath is designed to overflow so that it will be self-cleaning. Water flows in at the rate of <imath>20</imath> milliliters per minute and drains at the rate of <imath>18</imath> milliliters per minute. Which one of these graphs shows the volume of water in the birdbath during the filling time and continuing into the overflow time?
<asy>
size(450);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));
path[] p={origin--(8,8)--(14,8), (0,10)--(4,10)--(14,0), origin--(14,14), (0,14)--(14,14), origin--(7,7)--(14,0)};
int i;
for(i=0; i<5; i=i+1) {
draw(shift(21i,0)*((0,16)--origin--(14,0)));
draw(shift(21i,0)*(p[i]));
label("Time", (7+21i,0), S);
label(rotate(90)*"Volume", (21i,8), W);
}

label("$A$", (0*21 + 7,-5), S);
label("$B$", (1*21 + 7,-5), S);
label("$C$", (2*21 + 7,-5), S);
label("$D$", (3*21 + 7,-5), S);
label("$E$", (4*21 + 7,-5), S);
</asy>

<imath>\textbf{(A)}\ \text{A} \qquad \textbf{(B)}\ \text{B} \qquad \textbf{(C)}\ \text{C} \qquad \textbf{(D)}\ \text{D} \qquad \textbf{(E)}\ \text{E}</imath>

==Solution==
At first, the volume of water in the birdbath increases by <imath>20-18=2</imath> millimeters per minute.  Once the birdbath is full, the volume of water inside remains constant.  This is best represented by graph <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(A)}\ \text{A}}</imath>.

